# G044 — Remote Login

*v2. See `remote-login.md` for prose: identity verification as trust
establishment across a boundary, salted hashing as one-way trust,
sessions as time-bounded identity assertions, brute-force protection
as rate-limited `<-` gates, revoke-all as retroactive invalidation.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Agent Authentication

[agent-auth @agent @capability
    IN ORDER TO
        gate the operation behind three progressive checks
        so the expensive verb only runs after identity, role, and session
        have each passed — cheapest gate first

    @cred <- [auth[verify
                     {agent: @agent,
                      capability: @capability,
                      context: @current-choreography}]]
        || -> #no-such-credential

    @cred
        where valid is true                                ;; identity gate
        and role is in ["exec", "admin"]                    ;; role gate
        and session-remaining-seconds at least 60           ;; temporal gate
            -> [@agent[do {capability: @capability}]]
        where valid is false
            -> #unauthenticated
        where role is not in ["exec", "admin"]
            -> #unauthorised (identity proven, role insufficient)
        where session-remaining-seconds is less than 60
            -> #session-expiring (re-authenticate first)
]

---

## Verb Surface

[auth
    create-user         {username, password}    ;; stores salt + hash, not pw
    login               {username, password}    ;; || locked | bad-credentials
                                                ;; -> Session
    verify              {agent, capability, context}
    revoke              {session-token}
    revoke-all          {username}               ;; emergency kill
    attempts-remaining  {username}
    audit-log
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: salted hash `where` secret is never stored

[auth[create-user {username: @u, password: @p}]
    IN ORDER TO
        establish a verifier without ever storing @p itself
        so a database leak cannot reveal user passwords

    @salt <- [random[bytes {length: 16}]]
    @hash <- [hash[argon2id {password: @p, salt: @salt}]]
    store {username: @u, salt: @salt, hash: @hash}
    ;; @p is never written to disk, never kept in memory past this call
]

The verifier proves knowledge without storing the secret. Same
discipline G077's password safe applied internally — G044 applies it
across a trust boundary.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: lockout as `where` consecutive failures exceed threshold

[auth[login {username: @u, password: @p}]
    @user <- lookup @u
    @user
        where consecutive-failures at least @policy:max-attempts
          and (now - last-failure-ms) less than @policy:cooldown-ms
            -> #locked (rate-limited)
        where hash matches argon2id(@p, @user:salt)
            -> reset consecutive-failures to 0
            -> issue Session with expiry now + @policy:session-ttl
            -> append #login-success to audit-log
        otherwise
            -> increment consecutive-failures
            -> append #login-failure to audit-log
            -> #bad-credentials
]

Progressive cost: the first attempt is free; subsequent attempts
accumulate risk; the final attempt triggers lockout. Each failed
`<-` gate is a new ledger entry the audit log preserves.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: revoke-all `where` trust basis has changed

[auth[revoke-all {username: @u}]
    @sessions <- active sessions for @u
    for @s in @sessions
        mark @s as revoked (future verify returns #revoked)
    append #mass-revocation to audit-log
    IN ORDER TO
        invalidate every session retroactively when credentials
        are suspected compromised — the sessions were valid, they
        are now invalid because the underlying trust changed
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    [auth[create-user
           {username: "alice", password: "correct-horse"}]]

    @s <- [auth[login
                  {username: "alice", password: "correct-horse"}]]
    -> @s:token                      ;; -> opaque session token
    -> @s:expires-ms                 ;; -> now + ttl

    -> [auth[login
               {username: "alice", password: "wrong"}]]   ;; -> #bad-credentials
    -> [auth[login
               {username: "alice", password: "wrong"}]]   ;; -> #bad-credentials
    -> [auth[login
               {username: "alice", password: "wrong"}]]   ;; -> #locked

    [auth[revoke-all {username: "alice"}]]
    -> [auth[verify
               {agent: "alice", capability: "read",
                context: @c}]]        ;; -> #revoked
]
